1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,700
April twenty-third, fourteen fifty-three.

2
00:00:03,520 --> 00:00:03,640
Dawn.

3
00:00:04,260 --> 00:00:06,560
The defenders of Constantinople look down at the Golden

4
00:00:06,560 --> 00:00:09,040
Horn, the one harbor they were sure was safe…

5
00:00:09,680 --> 00:00:11,200
and it's full of enemy warships.

6
00:00:11,780 --> 00:00:12,280
Dozens of them.

7
00:00:12,740 --> 00:00:14,120
The giant chain guarding the entrance?

8
00:00:14,440 --> 00:00:14,900
Still there.

9
00:00:15,360 --> 00:00:15,740
Unbroken.

10
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:17,080
Nobody sailed past it.

11
00:00:17,580 --> 00:00:18,920
So how did the ships get in?

12
00:00:19,680 --> 00:00:21,640
They sailed over a hill.

13
00:00:22,400 --> 00:00:24,680
This is how a twenty-one-year-old sultan pulled off one

14
00:00:24,680 --> 00:00:26,480
of the boldest moves in military history.

15
00:00:29,300 --> 00:00:31,440
To understand why this was such a shock,

16
00:00:31,760 --> 00:00:33,220
you need to know what Mehmed the Second was

17
00:00:33,220 --> 00:00:33,760
up against.

18
00:00:34,380 --> 00:00:36,660
Constantinople had stood for over a thousand years,

19
00:00:36,840 --> 00:00:38,960
the last capital of the Roman Empire in the

20
00:00:38,960 --> 00:00:39,200
east.

21
00:00:39,980 --> 00:00:41,600
Water on two sides, and on the third,

22
00:00:41,840 --> 00:00:43,760
the most famous walls of the Middle Ages:

23
00:00:44,280 --> 00:00:45,640
a moat, an outer wall,

24
00:00:45,800 --> 00:00:47,040
and a massive inner wall,

25
00:00:47,040 --> 00:00:48,760
with nearly two hundred towers.

26
00:00:49,300 --> 00:00:51,600
No army had ever smashed through them.

27
00:00:52,280 --> 00:00:53,840
Mehmed brought around eighty thousand men,

28
00:00:53,840 --> 00:00:56,940
and a monster cannon built by a Hungarian engineer

29
00:00:56,940 --> 00:00:59,880
named Orban, firing stone balls that weighed more than

30
00:00:59,880 --> 00:01:00,860
half a ton.

31
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:04,710
Inside, Emperor Constantine the Eleventh had fewer than eight

32
00:01:04,710 --> 00:01:05,640
thousand defenders.

33
00:01:06,610 --> 00:01:08,230
But the city had a weak spot,

34
00:01:08,550 --> 00:01:09,790
and everyone knew it.

35
00:01:10,170 --> 00:01:13,290
Back in twelve oh-four, crusaders had taken Constantinople from

36
00:01:13,290 --> 00:01:15,490
the Golden Horn, where the sea walls were low.

37
00:01:16,090 --> 00:01:18,850
So this time, the Byzantines stretched a massive chain

38
00:01:18,850 --> 00:01:20,170
across the mouth of the harbor,

39
00:01:20,550 --> 00:01:22,870
floating on wooden logs, from the city to the

40
00:01:22,870 --> 00:01:23,730
tower of Galata.

41
00:01:24,430 --> 00:01:25,690
Behind it waited their ships.

42
00:01:26,310 --> 00:01:27,270
As long as the chain held,

43
00:01:27,650 --> 00:01:30,090
the harbor was safe, and every soldier could be

44
00:01:30,090 --> 00:01:31,010
sent to the land walls.

45
00:01:31,980 --> 00:01:33,420
And it held.

46
00:01:34,040 --> 00:01:35,980
Mehmed's fleet attacked it, and failed.

47
00:01:36,600 --> 00:01:39,380
Then, on April twentieth, four Christian supply ships fought

48
00:01:39,380 --> 00:01:41,940
their way straight through the entire Ottoman navy,

49
00:01:42,140 --> 00:01:44,020
while the whole city cheered from the walls.

50
00:01:44,640 --> 00:01:46,880
Mehmed was so furious that he rode his horse

51
00:01:46,880 --> 00:01:49,200
into the sea, shouting at his admiral.

52
00:01:49,580 --> 00:01:52,000
The chain was lowered, the four ships slipped inside,

53
00:01:52,320 --> 00:01:53,480
and the chain went back up.

54
00:01:53,900 --> 00:01:54,780
Two weeks into the siege,

55
00:01:55,160 --> 00:01:56,500
the young sultan was losing.

56
00:01:57,470 --> 00:02:00,730
So Mehmed made a decision that sounded insane.

57
00:02:01,150 --> 00:02:02,950
If his ships couldn't go through the chain…

58
00:02:03,390 --> 00:02:05,130
they would go around it.

59
00:02:05,570 --> 00:02:06,230
Over land.

60
00:02:06,850 --> 00:02:09,410
Behind the Genoese town of Galata rose a hill,

61
00:02:09,790 --> 00:02:11,750
and on the other side lay the Golden Horn,

62
00:02:12,190 --> 00:02:13,250
safely behind the chain.

63
00:02:13,930 --> 00:02:15,010
His men cleared a road,

64
00:02:15,350 --> 00:02:17,330
laid down wooden tracks and rollers,

65
00:02:17,590 --> 00:02:19,370
and greased them with animal fat.

66
00:02:20,150 --> 00:02:22,570
Dragging ships overland wasn't a brand-new idea.

67
00:02:22,830 --> 00:02:25,430
But over a hill, past a neutral town,

68
00:02:25,790 --> 00:02:26,850
in a single night?

69
00:02:27,470 --> 00:02:28,490
Nobody saw it coming.

70
00:02:29,460 --> 00:02:31,540
On the night of April twenty-second,

71
00:02:31,660 --> 00:02:32,220
the work began.

72
00:02:32,880 --> 00:02:34,820
One by one, ships were pulled out of the

73
00:02:34,820 --> 00:02:36,360
Bosphorus onto wooden cradles.

74
00:02:36,800 --> 00:02:39,200
Teams of oxen and thousands of men hauled them

75
00:02:39,200 --> 00:02:41,860
up the slope, the hulls creaking over the greased

76
00:02:41,860 --> 00:02:42,380
rollers.

77
00:02:43,140 --> 00:02:44,180
And here's the strangest part.

78
00:02:44,600 --> 00:02:46,900
According to the chroniclers, the sailors sat at their

79
00:02:46,900 --> 00:02:48,680
oars, rowing through the air,

80
00:02:49,020 --> 00:02:51,280
sails raised, as if they were already at sea,

81
00:02:51,560 --> 00:02:53,100
while drums and trumpets played.

82
00:02:54,040 --> 00:02:55,720
Ship after ship climbed the hill,

83
00:02:55,720 --> 00:02:57,320
and slid down the other side.

84
00:02:57,320 --> 00:03:00,740
By morning, around seventy ships were floating in the

85
00:03:00,740 --> 00:03:01,280
Golden Horn.

86
00:03:02,250 --> 00:03:03,830
The chain was never broken.

87
00:03:04,050 --> 00:03:05,350
It just didn't matter anymore.

88
00:03:05,790 --> 00:03:07,730
Now the defenders had to guard the harbor walls

89
00:03:07,730 --> 00:03:09,750
too, pulling men away from the land walls,

90
00:03:09,750 --> 00:03:11,130
where they were desperately needed.

91
00:03:11,690 --> 00:03:13,490
A Venetian night raid to burn the new fleet

92
00:03:13,490 --> 00:03:14,910
was betrayed, and failed.

93
00:03:15,510 --> 00:03:18,030
Then the Ottomans built a floating bridge across the

94
00:03:18,030 --> 00:03:18,210
harbor.

95
00:03:18,830 --> 00:03:20,770
On May twenty-ninth, fourteen fifty-three,

96
00:03:20,770 --> 00:03:22,450
came the final assault.

97
00:03:23,050 --> 00:03:24,630
The defenders held for hours,

98
00:03:24,630 --> 00:03:27,390
until their best commander, Giovanni Giustiniani,

99
00:03:27,570 --> 00:03:28,970
was wounded and carried away.

100
00:03:28,970 --> 00:03:30,290
The lines broke.

101
00:03:30,970 --> 00:03:32,650
The last emperor died fighting at the walls,

102
00:03:33,050 --> 00:03:35,310
and more than a thousand years of Roman history

103
00:03:35,310 --> 00:03:36,490
came to an end.

104
00:03:37,090 --> 00:03:38,710
The ships on the hill didn't win the siege

105
00:03:38,710 --> 00:03:39,090
alone.

106
00:03:39,490 --> 00:03:40,590
But in a single night,

107
00:03:40,590 --> 00:03:42,890
they turned a wall of water into just another

108
00:03:42,890 --> 00:03:43,250
front.

